item.content:encoded:
When Josh Wardle's Wordle went viral, it sparked an entire ecosystem of spinoffs and niche adaptations. While the instinct for modern web developers is to spin up  or a Next.js template for any interactive interface, a 5-letter word puzzle is one of the best use cases for ditching heavy frameworks entirely.Here is a quick breakdown of how I built a Canadian-themed Wordle clone () using only Vanilla JavaScript, plain HTML5, and CSS3—achieving sub-millisecond initial render times and zero external runtime dependencies.The Architecture: Zero-Backend StateWord puzzles need persistent state across page reloads without requiring an account or database infrastructure. Everything lives in the browser:[Game State Engine (Vanilla JS)] <----> [LocalStorage Engine]           [Word Validation & Color Match Logic]The app is built around three core pillars: A deterministic epoch-based daily selector. Handling exact matches, misplaced letters, and duplicate letter edge cases.Persistent State Management: Synchronizing tile states, keyboard colors, and streaks with .1. Deterministic Daily Word SelectionInstead of calling a backend API each midnight to fetch the "word of the day," the client computes the target word using an offset index based on a fixed launch date:const WORD_LIST = ["MAPLE", "MOOSE", "CABIN", "CANOE", "POUTN"]; // Curated themed list
const EPOCH_DATE = new Date("2026-01-01T00:00:00").getTime();

function getDailyTargetWord() {
  const now = new Date().getTime();
  const msPerDay = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
  const dayIndex = Math.floor((now - EPOCH_DATE) / msPerDay);

  // Modulo ensures list wrap-around without index overflows
  return WORD_LIST[dayIndex % WORD_LIST.length];
}
This guarantees every player across the globe sees the exact same puzzle on any given calendar day without a centralized database.2. The Duplicate Letter Trap in Word EvaluationThe hardest logic in a Wordle clone is not checking if a letter exists, but handling .If the target word is  and the player guesses :The first  is in the wrong spot (Yellow/Present).The second  is in the exact spot (Green/Correct).If you run a naive single-pass  check, you will incorrectly highlight both s even though the target only has two instances, often confusing the counts.To solve this in pure JavaScript, the algorithm runs in two distinct passes:function evaluateGuess(guess, target) {
  const targetChars = target.split('');
  const guessChars = guess.split('');
  const result = new Array(5).fill('absent'); // Default: Gray
  const targetCharCounts = {};

  // Count available letters in target
  for (const char of targetChars) {
    targetCharCounts[char] = (targetCharCounts[char] || 0) + 1;
  }

  // Pass 1: Mark exact matches (Correct / Green)
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (guessChars[i] === targetChars[i]) {
      result[i] = 'correct';
      targetCharCounts[guessChars[i]]--;
    }
  }

  // Pass 2: Mark misplaced letters (Present / Yellow)
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (result[i] !== 'correct') {
      const char = guessChars[i];
      if (targetCharCounts[char] > 0) {
        result[i] = 'present';
        targetCharCounts[char]--;
      }
    }
  }

  return result;
}
3. Lightweight State SerializationTo keep the game resilient against browser refreshes, the state updates to  on every valid row submission:const gameState = {
  currentStreak: 0,
  guesses: [],
  gameStatus: "IN_PROGRESS", // "WON", "LOST", "IN_PROGRESS"
  lastPlayedDate: new Date().toDateString()
};

function saveState() {
  localStorage.setItem("puzzle_save_v1", JSON.stringify(gameState));
}

function loadState() {
  const saved = localStorage.getItem("puzzle_save_v1");
  if (!saved) return;

  const parsed = JSON.parse(saved);
  // Reset board if day has changed
  if (parsed.lastPlayedDate !== new Date().toDateString()) {
    gameState.guesses = [];
    gameState.gameStatus = "IN_PROGRESS";
  } else {
    Object.assign(gameState, parsed);
    rehydrateUI();
  }
}
Why Vanilla JS Still Wins for Utility ProjectsBuilding this without a framework delivered tangible development benefits: The total production payload (HTML + CSS + JS) is less than 15 KB uncompressed. Zero , zero Webpack / Vite configuration bugs, and straightforward deployment to any static host. Keyboard layout bindings and flip animations are directly manipulated via  with zero virtual DOM overhead.For utility tools and browser games, stepping back from component abstractions and writing plain JavaScript keeps web projects fast, robust, and clean to maintain.

A connection drops and comes back. The dashboard goes green. What actually recovered?There are two answers, and they are not the same claim. Either the original transport survived an interruption, or something built a replacement, and the replacement is now doing the work. In most systems, the observable end state — connected, healthy, serving — is identical either way. The paths that produced it are not.This gets more pressing the more disposable your connections are, and modern designs make them very disposable. Peer connections get rebuilt. Pooled database handles get discarded wholesale. Consumers get their partitions reassigned. Leases expire and get reacquired by a different process. Treating the transport as inventory is the right call — it is what makes recovery cheap. But it opens a gap: if the thing carrying the session can be swapped at any moment, then the session is not the connection, and something has to say what it .The answer is unglamorous and load-bearing. A session is a name, plus a monotonically increasing counter, plus a rule about who checks them. Systems that state this explicitly get two things: recovery telemetry that means something, and immunity to a class of corruption where work issued by a dead transport lands on its replacement. Systems that leave it implicit get neither, and both failures are quiet.Three parts, and all three have to be present: that does not depend on whatever is carrying the session right now. — a counter that strictly increases every time the carrier is replaced. — whatever can be corrupted refuses work stamped with an older epoch.Drop the third, and you have a convention rather than a guarantee. That omission is the common one.The address is not the identityTCP identifies a connection by its endpoint addresses. That choice is why a laptop moving from Wi-Fi to cellular drops its connections: the addresses changed, so by definition it is not the same connection anymore. Identity was inferred from the transport's current circumstances, and the circumstances moved.QUIC's fix is worth studying because it is the entire pattern executed in one decision. A QUIC connection carries a set of connection IDs, and per RFC 9000 5.1, "the primary function of a connection ID is to ensure that changes in addressing at lower protocol layers (UDP, IP) do not cause packets for a QUIC connection to be delivered to the wrong endpoint." Section 9 states the payoff: "QUIC connections are not strictly bound to a single network path. Connection migration uses connection identifiers to allow connections to transfer to a new network path." The same paragraph notes that this "allows connections to continue after changes in network topology or address mappings, such as might be caused by NAT rebinding." Address changes the application never asked for stop being fatal, because identity was assigned rather than derived.The generalization is the useful part. Whatever carries your session right now is not allowed to be the thing that names it. Kafka expresses this as, a name the operator assigns that outlives any single producer process. Server-sent events express it as the  header: a reconnecting  sends its last event ID string so the server can resume the stream rather than restart it (WHATWG HTML). Different layers, same move.A name says which session; an epoch says which incarnationA stable name is necessary and not sufficient. The moment a session can be carried by a succession of transports, you need to distinguish  carrier from the one it replaced — because the one it replaced may still be alive, still holding a socket, still finishing an operation it started before anyone told it that it had been superseded.TCP has thought about this longer than most. RFC 9293 3.4.1 calls new instances of a connection "incarnations of the connection" and poses the question plainly: "how does the TCP implementation identify duplicate segments from previous incarnations of the connection?" The mitigations are the initial sequence number generator and, in 3.6.1, lingering in TIME-WAIT for twice the maximum segment lifetime. Both exist for one purpose — to keep bytes emitted by a dead incarnation from being accepted by its successor.Above the transport, the same idea shows up as an explicit counter: makes the transaction coordinator bump the epoch for the producer ID associated with a . KIP-98 describes the effect as fencing: "any previous zombie instance of the producer is fenced off and cannot move forward with its transaction." A zombie that keeps writing gets  from the coordinator, or, on the produce path since Kafka 2.7,  — "Producer attempted to produce with an old epoch." The split (KIP-588) exists so the second case can be aborted and retried rather than being fatal.Kafka consumer generation. Each rebalance increments a generation ID. A member that heartbeats or commits offsets against a stale one receives  — "Specified group generation id is not valid." The Raft paper is explicit that "terms act as a logical clock... they allow servers to detect obsolete information such as stale leaders." Figure 2 reduces it to two mechanical rules. Under "Rules for Servers": if an RPC request or response carries a term greater than, adopt it and convert to follower. And in the receiver implementation for both RPCs: "Reply false if term < ."Connection pool generation. MongoDB's CMAP specification gives each pool a generation number and stamps every connection with the generation in force when it was created. Clearing the pool does not walk the connection list; it increments the generation, which implicitly marks every existing connection stale. A connection whose generation does not match the pool's is closed on its next check-in or check-out.Unrelated layers, one shape:endpoint addresses — the weak pointreceiver, via the sequence window and TIME-WAITtransaction coordinator and partition leader plus member identityevery server, on every RPCthe pool, at check-in and check-outA monotonically increasing number that distinguishes the current incarnation from every earlier one, checked at the boundary where damage would otherwise occur. Note the last column: in every row, the party doing the checking is the one with something to lose.The check belongs to whoever can be corruptedThis is where implementations most often stop one step short. Generating the counter is easy. The question that decides whether it protects anything is .Martin Kleppmann's treatment of fencing tokens puts the requirement where it belongs: "this requires the storage server to take an active role in checking tokens, and rejecting any writes on which the token has gone backwards." A lock service that hands out strictly increasing tokens and a client that dutifully carries them buy you nothing if the resource being written accepts whatever arrives. Chubby had the same structure two decades ago — its sequencer carries a lock generation number, and "the recipient server is expected to test whether the sequencer is still valid... if not, it should reject the request." Chubby's  is the consolation prize for servers that will not do the check: hold the lock unavailable for a while after the holder disappears and hope the zombie has stopped.Worth knowing that this is not free by default in familiar infrastructure. The package documentation for Kubernetes'  leader election says so in one sentence: "This implementation does not guarantee that only one client is acting as a leader (a.k.a. fencing)." Winning the election tells you that you should act. It does not stop the previous leader from also acting.Inside a process, the discipline is two linesThe distributed case gets the literature, but the same hazard exists entirely within one process, where the "zombie" is a callback registered by an object you already threw away.Tag the carrier at construction and keep a counter alongside it. Any handler that can write into session state captures the epoch it was registered under and compares before it does anything:const epochAtStart = session.epoch;

pc.addEventListener("connectionstatechange", () => {
  if (epochAtStart !== session.epoch) return;   // stale callback, ignore
  session.record(pc.connectionState);
});
The second placement is less obvious and catches more. An asynchronous recovery routine can be superseded .  is the idiomatic tool, but its semantics are worth reading carefully.  runs synchronously. The WHATWG DOM Standard does require promise-based APIs to "convey that the operation got aborted by rejecting the promise with AbortSignal object's abort reason" — but that is an obligation on the API you called, and it says nothing about the frame that called it. Your own function resumes normally after the  and has to ask. Code that checked  before the  and not after has checked at the wrong moment:const ok = await attemptRecovery(signal);

// Ownership can change while the await is pending.
if (signal.aborted) return;          // or: signal.throwIfAborted()

if (!ok) await rebuildTransport(signal);
This is cheap to demonstrate, because it is a concurrency question rather than a connectivity one — an artificial delay opens the overlap window deterministically, with no network involved. In an open-source harness I built for recovery experiments (Chrome 151 and Firefox 153, driven by Playwright, with a local coturn relay), 20 trials with the post- ownership check enabled produced zero rebuild attempts from a superseded cycle; with the same check disabled, all 20 superseded cycles proceeded into a destructive rebuild. That is a forced race, not an estimate of how often the condition arises in production. What it shows is that when the window is open, the guard is the only thing between a superseded cycle and the next stage, and the outcome without it is not probabilistic. The overlap experiment is in the harness repository under an MIT license; it needs no relay and no interruption, so it runs anywhere in seconds.Before building network machinery to test failure handling, it is worth asking which of your failure scenarios are actually about the network. Several of the expensive ones are about ordering.Identity is what makes recovery measurableThe same tags pay for themselves in telemetry, and this is the part most teams are missing without knowing it."The session is connected again" and "the original transport recovered" are different claims, and a binary recovered/not-recovered metric cannot tell them apart. A freshly constructed carrier reaching a healthy state looks exactly like the old one returning. Counting both as recovery measures nothing about your recovery strategy — which is usually the thing you were trying to evaluate.Comparing identity at the moment of recovery costs one line:metrics.originalTransport = (transportIdAtFailure === transportIdNow);
With that field, questions that were previously unanswerable become routine. How often does the cheap recovery path actually preserve the existing transport, versus quietly escalating to a rebuild? Are your recovery attempts succeeding, or is your session merely being reconstructed repeatedly while every individual attempt reports success? The user-visible end state can be identical while the architecture took materially different paths, and only the identity comparison distinguishes them.Where the pattern stops helpingFour limits worth stating before you rely on this.An in-process epoch is a hint, not a fence. It protects state your process owns. It does nothing about a request already in flight to a service that does not check tokens. Real fencing requires cooperation from the resource, per Kleppmann's rule above.Monotonicity has to survive restarts. A counter that resets to zero when the process restarts is not monotonic, and a restarted instance will happily present epoch 1 to a peer that has already seen epoch 7. This is precisely why Kafka's producer epoch is owned and persisted by the transaction coordinator rather than by the client process.Guards prevent corruption, not committed side effects. Discarding a stale callback stops it from writing into the current session's state. It does not un-send the message the superseded cycle already published.Not every fence is an epoch fence. Kafka's  — "another consumer with the same  has registered with a different " — is a duplicate-identity check, not an ordering check. Conflating the two leads to expecting ordering guarantees from a mechanism that only detects collisions.Once you hold the shape, the individual mechanisms stop looking like unrelated trivia. TCP's TIME-WAIT and Kafka's producer epoch and Raft's terms and a connection pool's generation number are one design applied at different altitudes: assign the session a name that does not depend on its current carrier; attach a counter that strictly increases every time the carrier is replaced; and make whatever can be corrupted check that counter before it accepts work.Everything else — the socket, the peer connection, the pooled handle, the lease — is inventory. The interesting engineering question was never how to keep one connection alive. It is what you are willing to call the same session after the connection is gone.Jay Nirmal is a Senior Software Engineer based in the Boston area, working on backend and real-time communication systems. His technical interests include resilient real-time transport, recovery architecture, and observable distributed systems. The harness behind the controlled experiment in this article is open source at github.com/jaynirmal15/webrtc-recovery-harness.Iyengar, J., and Thomson, M., eds., QUIC: A UDP-Based Multiplexed and Secure Transport, RFC 9000, IETF, 2021 — 5.1 (connection IDs), 9 (connection migration). https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.htmlEddy, W., ed., Transmission Control Protocol (TCP), RFC 9293, IETF, 2022 — 3.4.1 (initial sequence number selection, connection incarnations), 3.6.1 (Half-Closed Connections; TIME-WAIT and 2xMSL). https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.htmlApache Kafka, KIP-98: Exactly Once Delivery and Transactional Messaging — producer ID and producer epoch. https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-98+-+Exactly+Once+Delivery+and+Transactional+MessagingApache Kafka, KIP-588: Allow producers to recover gracefully from transaction timeouts — the  /  split. https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-588:+Allow+producers+to+recover+gracefully+from+transaction+timeoutsApache Kafka,  — , , , . https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/trunk/clients/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/common/protocol/Errors.javaOngaro, D., and Ousterhout, J., In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm (Extended Version) — 5.1 and Figure 2, Rules for Servers. https://raft.github.io/raft.pdfMongoDB, Connection Monitoring and Pooling specification — pool generation and stale connections. https://github.com/mongodb/specifications/blob/master/source/connection-monitoring-and-pooling/connection-monitoring-and-pooling.mdKleppmann, M., How to do distributed locking, 2016 — fencing tokens. https://martin.kleppmann.com/2016/02/08/how-to-do-distributed-locking.htmlBurrows, M., The Chubby lock service for loosely-coupled distributed systems, OSDI 2006 — 2.4, sequencers and lock-delay. https://static.googleusercontent.com/media/research.google.com/en//archive/chubby-osdi06.pdfKubernetes, client-go/tools/leaderelection — package documentation on the absence of fencing. https://github.com/kubernetes/client-go/blob/master/tools/leaderelection/leaderelection.goWHATWG,  — 3, aborting ongoing activities; 3.3, using  and  objects in APIs. https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#abortcontroller-api-integrationWHATWG,  — server-sent events, the  header. https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.htmlNirmal, J.,  — Experiment 2, overlapping recovery race. https://github.com/jaynirmal15/webrtc-recovery-harness

I run a small, free tool called USFoodInJapan, a map that lets Americans traveling in Japan search a hotel, landmark, or neighborhood and instantly see every nearby US fast food chain and convenience store (konbini), with distance and one-tap directions. No account, no app install, just a search bar and a map. It's a static site with no backend, no framework, just HTML, vanilla JS, and Leaflet.js for the map layer.A few weeks ago, someone sent me a screenshot of the map with a giant "API KEY REQUIRED" watermark stamped across every tile. That kicked off a debugging story with a twist I didn't see coming: the fix I shipped first didn't just fail — it made the site worse. And the real cause turned out to be something that had been silently true for who knows how long before that.The map used two tile layers: Esri's World Street Map as the "primary" source, with a  handler falling back to CARTO's free raster basemap if Esri ever failed to load. Standard, boring, been running fine for months. Or so I thought.CARTO had recently started requiring an API key for their raster tile endpoint and serving a watermarked placeholder image to unauthenticated requests instead of erroring outright — a real policy change, not a fluke. Since the watermark was showing up, my first assumption was: CARTO (the fallback) is degrading, so let's point the fallback somewhere else — plain OpenStreetMap tiles instead.I shipped that. It made things . The map went from watermarked to completely blank./Diagnosis #2 (the real one)That regression was the tell. If swapping the fallback broke things further, the fallback must have been doing more work than I thought. I went back to the actual Content-Security-Policy header in the site's  file — and there it was: the  directive had never whitelisted , Esri's tile domain, at all. Ever.Which meant Esri — the tile source I'd built the site around, labeled as "primary" in the code — had likely been silently CSP-blocked in every browser since the day that header was written. Every real page load was hitting an instant  and falling straight through to CARTO, which had been quietly doing 100% of the actual work despite being coded as a mere fallback. When CARTO's own free tier started requiring a key, both paths were effectively broken at once — and my first "fix" pointed the still-broken fallback chain at yet another domain that also wasn't in the CSP allowlist, so  rendered.The actual fix was almost anticlimactic: add the missing domains to . Two lines. But the lesson stuck — a fallback that's silently propping up your "primary" system for months is a landmine, because the day it also breaks, you lose visibility into two failures at once and end up debugging the wrong one first.Turning the incident into an upgradeWhile researching CARTO's raster changes, I found their own docs saying raster basemaps are being phased out in favor of vector tiles — sharper rendering, no @2x asset doubling, restylable at runtime. I liked the look of their vector "Voyager" style better than Esri's anyway, so instead of just patching the CSP and moving on, I migrated the whole tile layer to vector.The tricky part: my map already had a working Leaflet setup: markers, popups, search, click-to-pin, and I didn't want to rewrite any of it just to swap tile rendering.  JS renders vector tiles, but it's not a Leaflet plugin by default. The fix was , a thin compatibility layer that lets a MapLibre GL vector layer live inside an existing Leaflet map instance as . Every line of marker/search/popup code kept working untouched, only the base tile layer changed.function addBaseLayer(targetMap) {
  const vector = L.maplibreGL({ style: CARTO_VECTOR_STYLE, attribution: CARTO_VECTOR_ATTR });
  vector.addTo(targetMap);
  vector.getMaplibreMap().on('error', () => {
    if (targetMap.hasLayer(vector)) targetMap.removeLayer(vector);
    L.tileLayer(ESRI_TILES, { attribution: ESRI_ATTR, maxZoom: 19 }).addTo(targetMap);
  });
  return vector;
}
This time, the fallback isn't hypothetical, it's wired to a real  event and I actually tested it, by pointing the style URL at a nonexistent domain and confirming Esri raster genuinely takes over. Given how the original incident played out, I wasn't going to ship another "fallback" I hadn't proven works.I also learned the CSP for vector tiles is a different directive than raster:  only governs -tag loads, but MapLibre fetches vector tile/style/sprite/glyph data via , which falls under . And the style JSON I was pointing at () actually serves real tile data from a  subdomain (tiles.basemaps.cartocdn.com). Easy to miss if you only whitelist the exact host you first tested against.None of this was exotic. It was a one-line CSP gap that had been silently redirecting all real traffic through an undocumented fallback path for an unknown length of time, discovered only because an  external service change happened to break the thing that was quietly propping everything up. If you maintain a fallback for anything, a tile layer, an API, a CDN, it's worth asking: do I actually know my primary path works right now, or do I just know the app renders?USFoodInJapan is free to use if you want to see the vector map in action, or poke around for chains near wherever you're headed in Japan.

In my last post, we designed a dual Roaring Bitmap inventory engine that mapped our GPU rack runtime availability status to a tiny payload. We set up Server-Sent Events (SSE), tested it on localhost, watched the latency plummet to sub-millisecond levels, and felt like we had beaten the system.Then, we released it to production.By Tuesday afternoon, our bandwidth bill spiked. Our elegant bitmap architecture had crashed into the realities of network scale. Here is the story of how our network layer broke. How we redesigned our subscription routing to handle the load.Our initial plan was simple: whenever a runtime's availability state changed on a GPU rack, serialize that rack's dual bitmaps and stream them to all active scheduling agents connected to that cluster zone via SSE.At scale, the math caught up with us.If a cluster zone has 10,000 active scheduling agents or edge nodes, and a runtime like Llama-3-70b runs out of free slots, we send a 100-byte payload to all 10,000 agents. That is 1MB. No big deal. But if we have 500 racks, and 10 runtimes change states every second under peak scheduler load, we are pushing:10 updates/sec×10,000 agents×100 bytes×500 racks≈5 GB/s10 updates/sec×10,000 agents×100 bytes×500 racks≈5 GB/sMost of those agents were executing static pipelines that didn’t even use the Llama-3 runtime. We were wasting CPU and bandwidth streaming updates no one cared about.The Fix: In-Memory Watchlist RoutingWe realized that scheduling agents only care about state changes for runtimes they are actively scheduling or monitoring.Instead of broadcasting the entire bitmap, we built a  on our orchestrator gateway nodes. When an edge agent opens an SSE connection, they register a dynamic subscription of runtime IDs they are currently utilizing or targeting.Edge Agent ---> Subscribe(Runtime: 10821, 44012) ---> SSE Connection Node
On the connection node, we maintain a thread-safe reverse index mapping runtime IDs to active agent sessions:import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArraySet;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.mvc.method.annotation.SseEmitter;

public class RackSubscriptionManager {

    // Maps Runtime ID -> Set of active agent SSE emitters
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, Set<SseEmitter>> runtimeWatchlist = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void watchRuntime(int runtimeId, SseEmitter emitter) {
        runtimeWatchlist.computeIfAbsent(runtimeId, k -> new CopyOnWriteArraySet<>()).add(emitter);
    }

    public void unwatchRuntime(int runtimeId, SseEmitter emitter) {
        Set<SseEmitter> emitters = runtimeWatchlist.get(runtimeId);
        if (emitters != null) {
            emitters.remove(emitter);
            if (emitters.isEmpty()) {
                runtimeWatchlist.remove(emitter);
            }
        }
    }

    /**
     * Pushes a lightweight delta update ONLY to interested agent nodes
     */
    public void notifyStateChange(int runtimeId, int bitmapIndex, String newState) {
        Set<SseEmitter> emitters = runtimeWatchlist.getOrDefault(runtimeId, Collections.emptySet());

        if (emitters.isEmpty()) return;

        // Tiny JSON payload instead of the entire bitmap
        String deltaPayload = String.format("{\"index\":%d, \"state\":\"%s\"}", bitmapIndex, newState);

        for (SseEmitter emitter : emitters) {
            try {
                emitter.send(SseEmitter.event().name("runtime-delta").data(deltaPayload));
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Connection died, clean up
                emitters.remove(emitter);
            }
        }
    }
}
Now, when our backend detects that a runtime slot availability state changes (e.g., from Healthy to Low Availability), we only publish a tiny delta {"index":42, "state":"LOW_AVAILABILITY"} to the handful of edge agents who actually have execution chains dependent on index 42.With this watchlist-driven subscription model, our network bandwidth dropped by  overnight. We were finally streaming data intelligently.We celebrated. Our connections were stable, and our network egress was tiny.But as any backend engineer knows, fixing one bottleneck simply exposes the next. As soon as our scheduling engines could see runtime availability in real-time, they started rushing to allocate the final remaining slots on candidate racks.Our network layer was saved. But our database was about to seize up entirely from write contention and row locks.In the next part, we'll talk about how we resolved DB hot-row locks during concurrent runtime allocations using a lock-free Redis reservation engine and append-only database ledgers.
